course: reflection
lesson: 14
title: When home feels shaky: rows, hospitals, new babies and big changes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Home is meant to be the steady place, so when it wobbles, with a row on Christmas Day, a mum in hospital, a new baby or a move nobody is sure about, a child can feel the ground shift under his feet.
explain: On Christmas Day Ellie's parents had a row and Mum cried in the kitchen; Ellie sat with her presents thinking, "Christmas is ruined. It's meant to be the best day. Why do they have to do it TODAY?", and for weeks Christmas songs made her feel flat.
explain: Holidays can be stressful for grown-ups, with money, cooking and family all at once, and one bad moment does not ruin every Christmas; a walk and hot chocolate on Boxing Day made a new happy memory, and the next December went fine.
explain: Not knowing is often the hardest part, so honest answers and asking straight questions help; and some things are never a child's fault or a child's job, like a court deciding about a parent, or old hurts between grown-ups.
explain: If home ever feels scary, like a parent throwing things in a temper, tell a trusted adult; it is not telling tales, it is how families get help.
fact: Holidays can be stressful for grown-ups because of money, cooking and family; one bad moment does not ruin every holiday after.
fact: Making a new happy memory, like a walk and hot chocolate the next day, can help after a spoiled day.
fact: Long hospital stays are scary for families; children need honest answers and someone to talk to, and imagining the worst alone makes it harder.
fact: Throwing things in a temper is scary and not okay; if you are scared at home, tell a trusted adult, and grown-ups can get help with their anger.
fact: After a split, parents sometimes say unkind things about each other ("bad-mouthing"); it is not fair on the child, who can love both parents.
fact: Teenagers sometimes run off when they feel overwhelmed; grown-ups and the police help find them, and it is not the younger sibling's fault.
fact: Parents can disagree about rules, like when a child gets a phone, without it being a sign of a big problem.
fact: Not knowing what will happen, for example about a job or a move, is hard; talking about it as a family helps more than guessing the worst.
fact: Grown-ups can have long histories and old hurts with each other ("old wounds"); children do not need to fix them.
fact: Sometimes a court decides that a child should not see a parent for a while, to keep the child safe; it is never the child's fault.
fact: New babies make everyone tired; it does not mean parents love the older children less, and jealousy is a normal feeling.
fact: Special time alone with a parent and helping with the baby can make an older child feel included again.
exercise: Ellie's parents rowed on Christmas Day. She decides Christmas will always be bad. What is true? | One bad moment doesn't ruin every Christmas | Christmas will now be ruined every year | Rows only happen on special days | Parents argue at Christmas because of the children
exercise: What helped Ellie after Christmas Day? | A Boxing Day walk and hot chocolate, a new happy memory | Deciding never to celebrate Christmas again, not ever, with anyone | Hiding in her room until January | Asking for twice as many presents next year
exercise: Ryan's mum is in hospital for two months and "nobody tells him anything". What helped him? | Visiting, daily calls and straight questions | Imagining the very worst all alone, every night | Pretending his mum was just away on a long holiday | Refusing to stay at his aunt's house while she was away
exercise: Zoe's dad lost his temper and threw a plate at the wall. What should Zoe do? | Tell a trusted adult, because it's scary and not okay | Keep it secret so Dad doesn't get into trouble | Be extra quiet forever at home so that he never gets cross again | Throw a plate too, to show him how it feels
exercise: What happened after Zoe told Mum? | Dad said sorry and got help with his anger | Zoe got told off for telling | Nothing changed at all | Dad moved to another country
exercise: Since the split, Mum calls Jacob's dad useless. Jacob thinks, "I'm half him. Am I useless too?" What helped? | Telling Mum it hurts when she runs Dad down | Agreeing with Mum so that she isn't upset any more | Refusing to go and visit his dad at weekends any more | Saying bad things about Mum to Dad to make it even
exercise: Amelia's big brother ran away for a night and the police came. What is true? | Teens sometimes run off when overwhelmed; not her fault | He ran away because Amelia was annoying and kept bothering him | Police only come if someone did a crime | He will definitely run away again
exercise: Finn's parents argued about whether he can have a phone. He thinks it's his fault for asking. What is true? | Parents can disagree about rules without a big problem | Asking for things always causes rows | He should never ask his parents for anything ever again | His parents are about to split up
exercise: Lucas's dad might lose his job and they might move. Lucas hates not knowing. What helped? | Asking questions and knowing the plan | Guessing the worst all alone every night | Refusing to talk about it with anyone at all | Packing his bag every evening just in case
exercise: Isla's grandpa yells at her mum when he visits, and Mum cries after. What is true? | Grown-ups have long histories; kids needn't fix them | Isla should tell Grandpa off herself | Her mum must have done something wrong to make Grandpa so cross | Grandpa yells because Isla is there
exercise: Oscar isn't allowed to see his dad after a court case. He thinks, "What did I do?" What is true? | Never the child's fault; courts act to keep kids safe | He must have done something to cause it | His dad doesn't want to see him at all | It means he did something against the law
exercise: What helped Oscar? | A counsellor, and letters to Dad kept for later | Thinking it was all his fault | Trying to find his dad on his own and visit him in secret | Never mentioning his dad again
exercise: A new baby arrived and Maddie thinks her parents love the baby more. What is true? | New babies make everyone tired; it doesn't mean less love | Parents always love the newest child the most, it's just nature | She should be naughty to get attention back | She will never get any attention again
exercise: What helped Maddie feel included? | Special Saturday time with Dad and helping with the baby | Being naughty so her parents noticed her | Ignoring the baby completely | Asking her parents to send the baby back
test: Ellie's cousin from Cardiff: "Christmas was proper lush till they started rowing, now it's ruined forever, innit." ("Lush" means lovely.) What's the truth? | One bad moment doesn't ruin every Christmas | She's right; it'll be ruined every year now | Christmas rows always happen again next year | Christmas is only lush for grown-ups
test: Ryan's aunt says, "Don't you worry about it, love," and won't say more. What would help Ryan more? | Honest answers he can understand | Never asking any more questions | Searching online for scary hospital stories | Guessing what's happening on his own
test: In a cartoon, a dad smashes plates when angry and everyone laughs. What's the truth in real life? | Throwing things is scary, not okay; tell a trusted adult | It's funny and harmless in every family, just like in the cartoon | Smashing plates is how grown-ups calm down | Children should copy it when they are angry
test: Jacob's mum says, "Your useless father." Jacob's nan, Irish: "Ah, sure, ye can love the both of them, pet." What does Nan mean? | He can love both parents, even after the split | He must choose just one of his parents to love from now on | His dad really is useless | His mum is wrong about everything
test: What would happen if Amelia decided her brother ran off because of her? | She'd carry blame that isn't hers | Her brother would come home faster | It would stop him running away again | Nothing; blaming yourself always helps
test: Finn's mate goes, "your parents arguing about your phone means they're splitting up, deffo." ("Deffo" means definitely.) Is that true? | No; parents can disagree about rules, that's all | Yes; any argument at all means they'll split up soon | Yes, if the argument is about a phone | No, because parents never argue
test: Lucas's American cousin says, "It's a total bummer not knowin', dude." ("Bummer" means a disappointing thing.) What helps most when things are uncertain? | Talking it over as a family and knowing the plan | Imagining every bad thing that could possibly happen | Pretending to everyone that nothing is changing at all | Refusing to eat anything until his parents decide
test: Isla says, "I'm gonna tell Grandpa off next time he yells at Mum!" What does the lesson say? | Grown-ups have old wounds; it isn't Isla's job to fix | Isla should shout at Grandpa to protect Mum | Isla should refuse to ever see Grandpa | Isla should make Grandpa and Mum hug
test: A kid says to Oscar, "you can't see your dad 'cause you were bad." What's the truth? | Courts act to keep kids safe; it's never the child's fault | The kid is right; it's because Oscar was bad | Oscar could see his dad again if only he behaved much better | It only happens to children who break rules
test: Maddie's mate, Gen Z: "the new baby's getting all the attention, it's giving left out." ("It's giving" means it feels like.) What helped Maddie in the end? | Special time with Dad and helping with the baby | Being naughty for attention every day | Hiding the baby's toys | Pretending the baby isn't there
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequence of a dispute between parents upon a festive occasion? | A single disagreeable incident does not determine the character of all future ones | Such an incident invariably renders all future festivities disagreeable | Festive disputes are caused principally by the children present | Festive occasions are wholly free of stress for adults
exam: It is given that a parent has thrown an object in anger. Determine the conduct appropriate to a frightened child. | Disclosure to a trusted adult | Concealment in order to protect the parent | Retaliation by the throwing of a further object | Silence pending the next such occurrence
exam: Identify the reason for which a court may determine that a child shall not meet a parent. | The protection of the child, without any fault of the child's | The misconduct of the child towards the parent | The child's failure to request such meetings | The expressed preference of the child's classmates
exam: Ascertain which statement is consistent with the facts concerning the arrival of a new infant in a family. | Parental fatigue does not signify diminished love for older children | Parents invariably love the youngest child most | Older children are required to withdraw from the family | Jealousy in older children is abnormal and unknown
